   "text": {
    "L1": "Rounding gives an about number, so estimates are quick and useful.",
    "L2": "Here is a common mistake: chopping off digits is not rounding.",
    "L3": "Look only at the digit just right of the place you are rounding to. Five or more rounds up; less than five rounds down.",
    "L4": "For 2,368 to the nearest hundred, the tens digit is 6, so it rounds up to 2,400.",
    "L5": "For 1,745 to the nearest hundred, the tens digit is 4, so it rounds down to 1,700.",
    "L6": "Now add the rounded numbers: 2,400 plus 1,700 is 4,100. The exact total is 4,113, so the estimate is sensible.",
    "L7": "Choose the rounding place that keeps the estimate useful, such as the nearest 100 kilometres for a trip plan.",
    "L8": "Remember, rounding changes the number we use for the estimate, not the real amount."
   },
